WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:08.960
We predicted that the Southern District of&nbsp;
New York prosecution, a district attorney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.760
would be a thorn in the side of Donald Trump&nbsp;
and his family long after he was out of office.&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:22.160
That may still be the case, but it is Georgia that&nbsp;
is jumping ahead very quickly and has launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:29.200
a criminal investigation into Donald Trump's&nbsp;
supposedly perfect phone call to Georgia officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.160
saying that they need to find eleven thousand&nbsp;
seven hundred and eighty votes for him. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.200
Donald Trump is under criminal&nbsp;
investigation. It's happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.680
even more quickly than some of us anticipated it&nbsp;
might happen. The New York Times reporting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.360
prosecutors in Georgia have started a criminal&nbsp;
probe into Donald Trump's attempts to overturn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.800
the election results in Georgia, including&nbsp;
that infamous phone call he made to Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.600
Secretary of State Brad Raffensperger. That's&nbsp;
the call where Trump insisted I only need eleven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.680
thousand seven hundred and eighty votes.&nbsp;
What are we going to do? Help me out here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:11.840
How can we find these votes? Fannie Willis, who&nbsp;
is the Democratic prosecutor in Fulton County,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.600
has now sent a letter to numerous officials in&nbsp;
state government, including Brad Raffensperger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.760
requesting that documents be preserved related&nbsp;
to an investigation into attempts to influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.000
the state's 2020 presidential election. So&nbsp;
this is notable for a few different reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.920
These notifications to preserve documents are&nbsp;
typical when there is an investigation that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:40.640
taking place, because this is a prosecutor in&nbsp;
Fulton County. We know that this is a criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.200
investigation. That's what it is. That's what it&nbsp;
means. That's what these prosecutors investigate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:53.600
Now, the letter does not mention Donald Trump by&nbsp;
name, but it is related to what Donald Trump did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.600
trying to change the outcome of Georgia's&nbsp;
election, insisting that these officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:05.920
should simply find these votes, make it happen.&nbsp;
And a copy of this letter that was sent was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.360
obtained by The New York Times. And it really&nbsp;
doesn't look good for Donald Trump. Now, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.680
speculation is Trump's ultimately going to be&nbsp;
charged with felony election fraud and that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.760
of the really important things to understand&nbsp;
is that when it comes to these statutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:30.080
even if you fail to convince someone to do the&nbsp;
thing you want them to do, it is still a crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.320
Quote, Under Georgia law, it is illegal to falsify&nbsp;
any records used in connection with an election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:43.440
or to place false entries in such records.
And any person who solicits requests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:51.680
commands, import tunes or otherwise attempts to&nbsp;
cause the other person to falsify election fraud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:57.440
in the first degree to falsify voting records&nbsp;
is guilty of criminal solicitation to commit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.800
election fraud in the first degree. This is a&nbsp;
felony offense. And much like with bribery, if.&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.960
You try to bribe someone and they don't accept&nbsp;
the bribe, you're still guilty if you I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.800
listen, when we talk about attempted murder, just&nbsp;
because you failed to kill someone doesn't mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:19.200
you've committed no crime and much the same way,&nbsp;
even if you fail to induce fraud by asking someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.680
in this case, Brad Raffensperger, to find these&nbsp;
eleven thousand seven hundred and eighty votes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:30.320
you are still culpable even if you don't get&nbsp;
them to do the thing you're asking them to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.360
Imagine if Georgia ruins Donald&nbsp;
Trump's life before New York. Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

